From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:27:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Port the multi-animal training loop and the EfficientNet
 helpers to TF2

core/train_multianimal.py is the multi-animal training loop: a
tf.compat.v1.Session driving a graph built once, fed by a FIFOQueue that a
Python thread fills through a Coordinator, optimised by
slim.learning.create_train_op and checkpointed by tf.compat.v1.train.Saver.
All 11 of its tf.compat.v1 references are that machinery.  This rewrites it
as an eager loop and removes every one of them.  nnets/utils.py loses 11 of
its 14; the 3 that remain are named below and cannot go on their own.

What replaces what, in core/train_eager.py -- new, and meant to be shared
with core/train.py when that loop is ported:

  make_dataset            the FIFOQueue(20), the 14 placeholders, the feeder
                          thread and the Coordinator become
                          tf.data.Dataset.from_generator(...).prefetch(20)
                          over the same PoseDatasetFactory.  The batches are
                          keyed by the *name* of each Batch member: tf.nest
                          sorts the keys of a dict it flattens and Batch
                          members do not compare, so a dict keyed by Batch
                          cannot cross a tf.function boundary.
  make_optimizer          MomentumOptimizer(lr, 0.9) keeps a <- 0.9a + g,
                          w <- w - lr a and SGD(lr, momentum=0.9) keeps
                          v <- 0.9v - lr g, w <- w + v, the same sequence for
                          a constant lr.  AdamOptimizer defaults to
                          epsilon=1e-8 and Keras to 1e-7, so it is passed.
  make_train_step         create_train_op makes GraphKeys.UPDATE_OPS control
                          dependencies of the loss, differentiates the
                          trainable variables, applies the gradients,
                          increments the global step and checks the loss for
                          inf and nan.  Calling the layers with
                          is_training=True applies those updates in place, so
                          the order is preserved and nothing is collected.
                          It does NOT add the regularization losses, and
                          neither does this: DLC registers weight decay into
                          GraphKeys.REGULARIZATION_LOSSES and never sums it
                          into total_loss.
  SnapshotSaver           Saver(max_to_keep=n).save(sess, prefix,
                          global_step=it) becomes save_tf1_checkpoint plus the
                          same retention policy and the same "checkpoint"
                          state file.  The snapshot stays name-based, so the
                          graph-mode code reads it unchanged.
  LearningRate            copied from core/train.py, which cannot be imported:
                          its line 34 disables eager execution for the whole
                          process at import time.

tf.compat.v1.train.Saver(slim.get_variables_to_restore(...)) becomes
nnets/checkpoint.load_tf1_checkpoint with include_prefixes, which is the same
filter; remove_head passes the model's own non-pose prefixes.  The redownload
that auxfun_models.smart_restore performs is kept in restore_weights.
tf.compat.v1.summary.{scalar,merge_all,FileWriter} become tf.summary; the
ConfigProto/allow_growth dance becomes tf.config.experimental.

Equivalence, measured on a real multi-animal project (openfield, 4 body
parts, 6 limbs, PAF prediction on, resnet_50, batch size 8, Adam, lr 1e-4),
both engines resuming the same snapshot-200 and running 20 iterations of the
real multi-animal-imgaug pipeline from the same seed:

  loss trajectory        identical at every one of the 20 iterations, to the
                         4 decimals learning_stats.csv records
  snapshot               271 tensors in both, the same names; the eager one
                         adds global_step, which Saver.restore ignores
  weights after 20 steps |w220 - w200| max 2.026e-03 mean 1.260e-04 in both;
                         |tf1 - tf2| max 6.886e-04 mean 1.830e-06, i.e. 1.5%
                         of the movement on average and 4.9e-05 of the mean
                         weight.  Adam divides by sqrt(v), so a float32
                         difference in the gradient is amplified wherever the
                         gradient is near zero; 766 of 24003544 scalars move
                         in one run and not the other.
  restore                predict.setup_pose_prediction reads the snapshot the
                         eager loop wrote and runs; the graph-mode training
                         graph's own Saver restores all 271 of its variables
                         with max |restored - checkpoint| = 0.

Batch norm, the thing a naive GradientTape drops.  Same project as
DLCRNet/MuNet (stride 4, multi_stage, batch size 2) from the ImageNet
resnet_v1_50 checkpoint, 10 iterations:

  decoder moving_mean      1280/1280 scalars moved, max |x - 0| 0.036 (tf1)
                           and 0.033 (eager)
  decoder moving_variance  1280/1280 scalars moved, max |x - 1| 0.0092 (tf1)
                           and 0.0090 (eager)
  backbone moving_mean/variance  0 of 53120 scalars moved, in both engines

The last line is the control: multi.py builds the resnet backbone with
is_training=False even while training, so its 106 moving averages are frozen
in the graph-mode loop too.  The eager loop reproduces both halves -- the
decoder's statistics track, the backbone's do not -- which is what shows the
update mechanism survived rather than that something is simply moving.

nnets/utils.py.  build_learning_rate's exponential_decay is
keras.optimizers.schedules.ExponentialDecay, which is what
tf.compat.v1.train.exponential_decay calls in graph mode; the five
tf.compat.v1.logging.info and the one .fatal are tf.get_logger();
tf.compat.v1.div is tf.math.divide for the float input drop_connect gives it.
build_optimizer's three optimizers become their keras.optimizers.legacy
counterparts, except that tf.compat.v1.train.RMSPropOptimizer initialises its
mean-square accumulator to one and Keras initialises it to zero, which is a
different optimiser and not a rounding difference: 50 steps on a quadratic
diverge by 6.3e-01 without the RMSProp subclass here and are bit-identical
with it.  TpuBatchNormalization._moments gains the mask argument Keras has
passed since 2.13; without it the class raises TypeError the moment it
normalises with batch statistics, so it has been unusable, and the one place
that would build it is commented out.

Per function, against the pre-port module in the same graph-mode process:
batch_spec, wrapper, the gaussian kernel, all four learning-rate schedules
over 3000 steps, both batch-norm classes (variable names, the update_ops they
publish, outputs and moving averages over 6 batches), drop_connect and
DepthwiseConv2D are bit-identical; sgd and rmsprop are bit-identical over 50
steps and momentum differs by 5.96e-08, two float32 ulp at 0.27.

The three tf.compat.v1 references left in nnets/utils.py are the base classes
of TpuBatchNormalization, BatchNormalization and DepthwiseConv2D, and they
are load-bearing for EfficientNet's variable names.  backbones/
efficientnet_model.py builds the rest of that network from
tf.compat.v1.layers.Conv2D and tf.compat.v1.variable_scope inside a
tf.keras.Model; a tf.compat.v1 layer takes its name from the variable scope
and a plain Keras layer takes it from the enclosing Keras model, so swapping
only these three renames every batch-norm and depthwise variable of the
backbone, inserting a "model/" component the convolutions around them do not
get: efficientnet/efficientnet-b0/blocks_0/tpu_batch_normalization/gamma
becomes efficientnet/efficientnet-b0/model/blocks_0/tpu_batch_normalization/
gamma, and depthwise_conv2d is uniquified differently.  Measured on the whole
EfficientNet-B0 pose net: 308 variables, none of the names shared with the
graph the pretrained checkpoint was written for.  They can only move together
with the rest of that backbone.  With them left alone that net is unchanged:
308 variables, 212 trainable, 96 update_ops, identical names, and the three
heads and the moving averages bit-identical, with and without
use_batch_norm.
